Yes, a projection mapping app for smartphones is not only possible, it is already a rapidly growing reality in the app market right now. With the processing power of modern smartphones (especially their GPUs) and advancements in mobile camera tracking, apps are successfully bringing projection mapping out of the realm of bulky laptops and expensive software.Here is a breakdown of how smartphone projection mapping apps are working today and the major players currently doing it.How Smartphone Mapping Apps WorkModern smartphones handle projection mapping by bypassing the need for a separate computer. The workflow generally looks like this:Connection: You connect your phone or tablet directly to a standard projector using an HDMI adapter (like a USB-C to HDMI cable) or wirelessly via Apple AirPlay or Google Cast.Surface Setup: Using the phone's touchscreen, you drop pins and drag corners (mesh warping and keystone correction) to align your digital canvas exactly with the physical object or wall you are projecting onto.Content Delivery: The apps allow you to load your own videos/photos from your camera roll, or they provide built-in visualizers, animations, and sound-reactive graphics. The phone's GPU renders the warped image in real-time and sends the clean, perfectly aligned output to the projector.   Advanced Features: Some apps are even using the phone's camera and AR capabilities to enable features like "Live Object Detection" or 3D portal effects that track the position of a viewer's head.   Leading Apps Currently on the MarketIf you want to see what the competition looks like or just try it out, here are the most prominent apps currently available for iOS and Android:Lazy Lighting (iOS): This is one of the most popular and highly-rated options for Apple devices. It focuses on making projection mapping accessible and intuitive. Users can map surfaces, apply built-in visuals or their own media, and even export the finished show to play later without the phone connected.   ProMapper (iOS & Android): A very comprehensive app that positions itself as a professional tool without the computer. It features real-time GPU-powered fluid effects, multi-surface mapping, timeline management for sequencing shows, and advanced tools like a surface slicer and animated typography. It even supports 3D portals with head tracking.   SurfaBeam (Android & iOS): A strong contender, especially for Android users where options have historically been more limited. SurfaBeam offers precision corner pinning, Bezier edge warping, custom shape editing, and multi-surface support. It is highly praised for being lightweight and not requiring invasive device permissions.   The Pros and Cons of the Mobile ApproachWhile mobile apps are fantastic, they do have distinct advantages and limitations compared to traditional desktop software like HeavyM, Resolume, or MadMapper.   The Advantages:Portability: You can set up an installation anywhere with just your phone, a projector, and a cable.Ease of Use: Touch interfaces are incredibly intuitive for dragging corner pins and drawing masks compared to using a mouse.Cost: Mobile apps are significantly cheaper (often ranging from free to $30 for pro features) compared to desktop suites that can cost hundreds of dollars.The Limitations:Multi-Projector Setups: If you want to use three projectors stitched together with edge-blending for a massive building facade, a smartphone will struggle to output multiple separate video streams simultaneously. You generally need a desktop with a dedicated graphics card for that.Storage and Processing: Rendering multiple layers of 4K video simultaneously can heat up a phone quickly and drain the battery. Heavy, complex installations still rely on dedicated hardware.A smartphone app is highly viable for smaller projects, DJ sets, art installations, holiday decorations, and quick site tests. It completely democratizes projection mapping by removing the barrier of entry.